<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turin King List</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Turin Royal Canon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> King List, or Turin Royal Canon, is an Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and now in the Museo Egizio in Turin. It lists gods and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with their reign lengths, including many minor rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a> omitted from other lists. Badly fragmented, it has been reconstructed by modern scholars, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-ryholt">Kim Ryholt</a>, whose work identified the Sixteenth and Abydos dynasties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turin-king-list.jpg" alt="Turin King List" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Il_cosiddetto_%27Papiro_dei_Re_o_Liste_dei_Re%27_2_C1874vs_3.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Turin King List is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> thought to date from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>, who ruled from 1279 to 1213 BCE. It is the most extensive list of kings compiled by the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and the basis of most Egyptian chronology before his reign. The list is written on the back of a discarded tax register, which may mean the writer did not treat it as formally important; its main function is thought to have been as an administrative aid.</p><p>Its beginning and end are lost, with no introduction and nothing after the 19th Dynasty, so it may have been composed at any time from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> to as late as the 20th Dynasty. It is a sloppy copy of a more detailed original that rested on much older sources. Only the kings of the First and Second dynasties have both their length of reign and age at death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> is divided into eleven columns. The first two hold the gods, spirits and mythical kings; the human kings follow, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> to the 17th, and perhaps once a twelfth column carried the 18th and 19th. It gives the length of each reign in years, with months and days for some, and sometimes groups rulers by family, roughly as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>'s dynasties do. It includes ephemeral rulers and those of small territories who are unmentioned elsewhere.</p><p>It also lists the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> kings of the 15th Dynasty, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> delta. They have no cartouches, and a sign marks them as foreigners, although foreign rulers are not usually listed in king lists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-ryholt">Kim Ryholt</a> estimates that the list once held about 250 kings, with about 270 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menes">Menes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> over some 1,800 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The circumstances of discovery are unknown. The Italian traveller Bernardino Drovetti bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> at Thebes around 1818. It was purchased at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> in 1820 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1824, where the Egyptian Museum acquired it as Papyrus Number 1874. When its box was opened, it had crumbled into small fragments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion">Jean-François Champollion</a> drew what he could read, and Gustav Seyffarth rebuilt it from the fibres of the papyrus alone, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> could be read.</p><p>Giulio Farina published a new placement of fragments with a transcription, translation and commentary in 1938, and Ryholt published a new interpretation in 1997. Donald Redford has noted discrepancies between its names and monuments and other documents. About half the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> is still missing; it is now 1.7 m long and 0.41 m wide in over 160 fragments, and unpublished fragments were found in the museum's storage in 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Turin King List” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yet the <b>Turin King List</b> records several dozen kings in about a century and a half, many with reigns of a few years or less, which suggests that real power lay increasingly with viziers and court families.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1997 the Egyptologist Kim Ryholt proposed that a group of kings in the <b>Turin King List</b> ruled a local kingdom at Abydos around 1650 BCE, after the collapse of the Middle Kingdom of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/abydos-dynasty">Abydos Dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Its existence as a separate dynasty was clarified by the reconstruction of the <b>Turin King List</b> by Kim Ryholt.</blockquote><a href="/state/sixteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Sixteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
